# g85-fu-node-diff (core): what the lead applies after the wave.
#
# A-5 (provider): Node's TLS client trusts the bundled Mozilla roots; the core's reqwest trusts the OS store only
# (`rustls-tls-native-roots`), so a machine whose store lacks a root Node accepts fails the handshake in the core.
#
# 1. native/core/Cargo.toml line of `reqwest = {...}` (workspace dependencies): add "rustls-tls-webpki-roots" to `features`, keeping
#    "rustls-tls-native-roots" (both roots are then trusted, nothing in use is narrowed):
#
#        reqwest = { version = "0.12", default-features = false, features = ["charset", "cookies", "http2", "macos-system-configuration", "rustls-tls-native-roots", "rustls-tls-webpki-roots"] }
#
# 2. native/core/Cargo.lock: the lock gains a `webpki-roots` 1.x entry (the cargo registry cache holds 1.0.5, 1.0.10 and 1.0.11 sources, so
#    an offline `cargo update -p reqwest --offline` or `cargo metadata --offline` writes it); the `--locked` build cannot gain it itself.
#
# 3. native/core/crates/mixdog-workspace-hack/Cargo.toml (hot file, hakari-managed): its `reqwest = {...}` line gains the same
#    "rustls-tls-webpki-roots" feature after "rustls-tls-native-roots" so `cargo hakari verify` stays clean (or run `cargo hakari generate`):
#
#        reqwest = { version = "0.12", default-features = false, features = ["brotli", "charset", "cookies", "deflate", "gzip", "http2", "json", "macos-system-configuration", "rustls-tls-native-roots", "rustls-tls-webpki-roots", "socks", "stream"] }
#
#    Not covered here: the tokio-tungstenite websocket clients (mixdog-ai, mixdog-mcp, mixdog-remote, mixdog-services, mixdog-tools) still
#    trust the OS store only; Node's ws uses the bundled roots there too.
